# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CRLF pins for converter-emitted artifacts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# go2cs emits CRLF UNCONDITIONALLY — the line ending is written into the output by
# the emitters themselves (initOrderOperations.go, projectFileWriter.go,
# packageInfoWriter.go, readme.go, solutionGenerator.go, autoSiblingOperations.go,
# moduleConverter.go, packageStateOperations.go, testConversion.go), never taken
# from the host. That makes the converter byte-deterministic across platforms and
# leaves the CHECKOUT as the only variable: with no attribute, a Windows clone
# (core.autocrlf=true) materializes CRLF while a Linux/macOS clone (autocrlf=false,
# the platform default) materializes LF. The converter then rewrites every file it
# regenerates and `git status` reports the WHOLE corpus as modified — so CNR, the
# authoritative drift instrument, is 100% false-positive on a non-Windows clone
# before a single line of converter work (F2 in docs/PLAN-linux-operation.md).
#
# Pinning eol=crlf makes every checkout — Windows, Linux, macOS, autocrlf on or off
# — materialize the exact bytes the converter regenerates, so a post-transpile
# `git status` is a truthful drift signal everywhere. Blobs stay LF in the object
# store (`text` normalizes on check-in), which is what they already are, so this pin
# does NOT rewrite the index: `git add --renormalize .` over all 9,380 tracked files
# stages nothing but this file. It makes the Windows lane's existing end state
# explicit instead of dependent on a per-machine core.autocrlf.
#
# ORDER MATTERS: these patterns must stay ABOVE the -text blocks below, because the
# last matching pattern wins. -text keeps the verbatim-bytes exemption for the four
# runtime-byte-sensitive behavioral projects and the testdata trees: an explicitly
# UNSET text attribute suppresses all conversion on both check-in and check-out, so
# the eol value those paths inherit from this block is inert. (`git check-attr eol`
# still ECHOES crlf for them — measured on git 2.42, where a trailing `!eol` does
# not override an earlier `eol=`. Read `text: unset` as the operative answer; the
# proof that nothing converts is that renormalize leaves all 54 -text paths, which
# are the only CRLF/mixed blobs in the index, untouched.)
*.cs         text eol=crlf
*.cs.auto    text eol=crlf
*.cs.target  text eol=crlf
*.csproj     text eol=crlf
*.slnx       text eol=crlf
*.props      text eol=crlf
*.targets    text eol=crlf
src/core/**/README.md text eol=crlf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LF pin for shell scripts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The mirror image of the block above, for the one file type where the CHECKOUT
# form is not cosmetic but a hard runtime requirement: a POSIX shell reads the
# shebang literally, so a CR at the end of line 1 makes the interpreter name
# `sh\r` and the script dies before it runs --
#   $ ./src/tour/scripts/start.sh -runtime=nuget
#   /usr/bin/env: 'sh\r': No such file or directory        (exit 127)
# The damage is not confined to the shebang: every trailing CR survives into
# command substitution, so `tour_root=$(... && pwd)` and `export GO_TOUR_BIN=`
# would carry CRs into paths even if the interpreter resolved.
#
# With no attribute these files inherited autocrlf's default text handling and
# materialized CRLF on any Windows clone (core.autocrlf=true is this repo's
# working default), breaking the README's documented "Start on macOS or Linux"
# path -- ./src/tour/scripts/start.sh -- for WSL, containers and a Linux CI lane
# alike. Blobs are already LF in the object store, so this pin does NOT rewrite
# the index (`git add --renormalize '*.sh'` stages nothing); it makes the ONE
# correct checkout form explicit instead of dependent on a per-machine setting.
#
# ORDER: above the -text blocks, same as the CRLF pins, so a byte-sensitive
# testdata fixture that happens to be a .sh keeps its verbatim-bytes exemption
# (src/core/**/testdata/** below). There are no such fixtures today; the
# ordering costs nothing and keeps the file's one rule -- last match wins,
# -text has the last word -- intact.
*.sh text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LF pin for the tables the shard-map generator PARSES
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Same reasoning as the shell-script pin above, for a different consumer: these
# are DATA files read byte-wise by docs/phase4/hopA-inputs/shardmap.py, which
# refuses a CR outright rather than normalizing on read.
#
# The refusal is deliberate and it must not be softened -- a generator that
# silently accepts either line ending cannot tell a table it parsed from a table
# it half-parsed, and this one's whole job is to be exact about row content (it
# checks a sha256 over `path\tt_r` pairs). But a guard is only as good as its
# premise, and the premise was FALSE on one platform: the committed blobs are LF,
# while a Windows checkout under core.autocrlf=true (this repo's working default)
# materialized them as CRLF, because no rule here covered docs/phase4/*.md -- the
# markdown pin above is scoped to src/core/**/README.md. So the guard would have
# fired on a correct Windows checkout and read as a corrupted table.
#
# Ruled 2026-09-13 (coordinator, mailbox `dfd85f700`): strengthen the premise,
# never weaken the guard. Blobs are already LF, so this pin rewrites nothing
# (`git add --renormalize` stages nothing); it makes the one correct checkout form
# explicit instead of per-machine. The `eol` attribute governs `git archive` the
# same way, so an exported tree carries it too.
#
# ORDER: above the -text blocks, for the reason the two pins above state.
docs/ValidatedTestPackages.md text eol=lf
docs/phase4/DATA-sweep-row-walltimes.md text eol=lf
docs/phase4/DATA-h10-shardmap-projection-go124.md text eol=lf

# 2026-09-22, H10 closing-arithmetic seat: the SAME ruling, the inputs it missed.
# shardmap.py CR-guards FIVE files, not two -- the roster and the walltime basis
# (pinned above), plus `recon-lists/*.txt`, `relocations.tsv` and whatever TSV
# `--timings` names (recon-basis.tsv for the H10 pass-1 plan), none of which
# carried a rule. Measured on a Windows checkout of this branch: population.txt
# 255 CR bytes, relocations.tsv 14, recon-basis.tsv 214 -- every one of them LF
# in the object store. The generator therefore REFUSED at its own population read
# before reaching any later check, and has been unrunnable on a correct Windows
# checkout since those guards landed; the refusal reads as a corrupted input and
# is a correct checkout. Strengthen the premise, never weaken the guard
# (2026-09-13). Blobs are already LF, so this pin rewrites nothing
# (`git add --renormalize` over the directory stages nothing).
docs/phase4/hopA-inputs/recon-lists/*.txt text eol=lf

# 2026-09-29, S4: -Hop's machine-read inputs (genpopulation's population-<release>.txt and the
# relocations-<release>.tsv table). The same reason as the line above: a CR rides into a package name.
docs/phase4/data/*.txt text eol=lf
docs/phase4/data/*.tsv text eol=lf
docs/phase4/hopA-inputs/*.tsv text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LF pin for the converter's embedded stdlib metadata asset
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Same reasoning as the three pins above, for the asset genstdlibmeta writes and
# TestStdLibMetadataInSync guards. The generator used to emit CRLF while the blob
# is LF, so a regeneration's RAW diff read 5,573 changed lines where the real
# content delta was 339 -- every other line differing on endings alone. The
# generator now writes LF (internal/stdlibmeta/generate.go), and this pin is what
# keeps the checkout form from depending on a per-machine core.autocrlf.
#
# The guard itself is ending-insensitive (it compares through
# normalizeLineEndings), so this pin is not what makes it pass -- it is what keeps
# a future reader's `git diff` legible.
#
# Blobs are already LF, so this rewrites nothing: `git add --renormalize` stages
# nothing beyond the files this commit edits.
#
# ORDER: above the -text blocks, for the reason the pins above state.
src/go2cs/stdlib-metadata.txt text eol=lf

# go2cs behavioral TargetComparison tests compare transpiled C# to .cs.target
# golden files BYTE-FOR-BYTE (TargetComparisonTests.FileMatch). The converter
# preserves the Go source's LF inside multi-line string literals, so these files
# legitimately contain mixed CRLF/LF. With core.autocrlf=true git would rewrite
# those LFs to CRLF on checkout and break the byte comparison. Mark the affected
# projects' transpiled output and goldens as binary so their exact bytes survive.
src/tests/Behavioral/Solitaire/*.cs            -text
src/tests/Behavioral/Solitaire/*.cs.target     -text
src/tests/Behavioral/SortArrayType/*.cs        -text
src/tests/Behavioral/SortArrayType/*.cs.target -text
src/tests/Behavioral/StdLibInternalAbi/*.cs        -text
src/tests/Behavioral/StdLibInternalAbi/*.cs.target -text
src/tests/Behavioral/UntypedConstWideMask/main.cs -text
src/tests/Behavioral/UntypedConstWideMask/main.cs.target -text

# A captured run artifact banked as EVIDENCE, not as generated output. The board keeps it
# because the reading is not reproducible on demand (crypto/tls TestBogoSuite shape (b) needs
# Go's own BoGo baseline to fail), so its exact bytes are the point: -text stops autocrlf from
# rewriting the line endings of the file whose whole value is being the run that happened.
docs/phase4/evidence-*.json -text

# Phase-4 test DATA staged from GOROOT and read byte-for-byte by a converted test
# suite. Go ships these with LF; autocrlf would smudge them on checkout and change
# what the test reads (compress/gzip's TestIssue6550 base64-decodes this file).
src/core/**/testdata/*.base64 -text
# Marking the whole testdata tree also stops a validated-package sweep from
# reporting these fixtures as modified on every run.
src/core/**/testdata/** -text
# Every //go:embed payload OUTSIDE testdata/ that a converted csproj names as an
# <EmbeddedResource Include>, derived from every tracked and every emitted csproj at the
# H10 close (2026-09-23). Go embeds these bytes with LF; under core.autocrlf=true an
# unpinned text payload checks out CRLF and the embedded resource is no longer Go's.
src/core/internal/trace/traceviewer/static/** -text
src/core/embed/internal/embedtest/concurrency.txt -text
src/core/crypto/internal/fips140test/acvp_capabilities.json -text

# The converter EMBEDS these templates at compile time (embeddedTemplates.go) and
# assumes CRLF: writePackageInfoFile splits the package_info skeleton on "\r\n", so
# a converter built from an LF checkout (Linux/macOS, or Windows with autocrlf=false)
# never finds the section end-markers and log.Fatals on EVERY package conversion —
# and the emitted .csproj/package_info.cs bytes must not vary with the build host's
# checkout. Pin them to CRLF in every working tree.
src/go2cs/csproj-template.xml      eol=crlf
src/go2cs/test-csproj-template.xml eol=crlf
src/go2cs/package_info-template.txt eol=crlf
